LEARNING LOG — Playful Widgets API (Go + REST + WS + Redis)
Project: api-server (blog widgets backend, learning-focused)
Mentor-guided build. Log kept in reverse-chronological-topic order (by feature, not by date).

================================================================
1. PROJECT SETUP — walking skeleton
================================================================
- Built /health first: pings Redis, proves the whole chain (HTTP -> Go -> Redis) works
  before adding real features.
- Bugs found & fixed:
  - Missing `return` after writing an error response -> fell through and wrote a
    second JSON body to the same ResponseWriter (double-write, wrong status code).
  - Missing explicit `w.WriteHeader(500)` on the error path -> client always saw 200
    even when Redis was down. Lesson: default HTTP status is 200 unless you say
    otherwise.
  - Used `context.Background()` for the Redis call instead of `r.Context()` -> Redis
    call wouldn't cancel if the client disconnected.
  - No timeout on the Redis call at all -> could hang forever if Redis was slow but
    not fully down. Fixed with `context.WithTimeout(r.Context(), ...)`.
- Concept: default to no timeout/no cancellation in Go network calls unless you wire
  it up yourself — nothing does it for you.

================================================================
2. ANONYMOUS IDENTITY — HMAC-signed cookie + middleware
================================================================
- Design: signed cookie (anonID + "." + HMAC-SHA256 signature), issued if missing,
  verified on every request, treated as "identity" for cap tracking.
- Concept: HMAC proves a value came from the server (has the secret) and wasn't
  tampered with. Plain hashing doesn't — anyone can hash, only the server can sign.
- Bugs found & fixed (roughly in order found):
  - Nil pointer panic: `r.Cookie()` returns `(nil, ErrNoCookie)` on a missing cookie —
    used `cookie.Value` on the nil pointer anyway in a later code path.
  - Verified the ID portion against a hex-decode, but the ID was never hex-encoded in
    the first place (only the signature was) — verification always failed for real
    cookies.
  - Unchecked slice index after `strings.Split` — a cookie value with no "." in it
    (attacker-controlled input) caused an index-out-of-range panic.
  - Cookie was being "set" as a raw string via `Set-Cookie` header instead of
    `http.SetCookie` + `http.Cookie{}` — missing `Secure`/`HttpOnly`/`SameSite`/MaxAge
    entirely, and not even correctly named (no `anonid=` prefix).
  - `r.WithContext(...)`'s return value was discarded (called but not reassigned) —
    context propagation silently did nothing for several iterations. `WithContext`
    returns a *new* request; the original is untouched.
  - The verified anon ID was silently discarded (`_, ok := verifyAnonID(...)`) on the
    *common* path (valid returning cookie) — only the exceptional paths even attempted
    propagation, and those were broken by the bug above too.
  - Secret key was never actually set on `Application` for several rounds — HMAC ran
    against a nil/empty key, meaning signatures were trivially forgeable.
  - `godotenv.Load()`'s error was silently swallowed via variable-reuse before being
    checked deliberately.
- Concept: constant-time comparison (`hmac.Equal`) instead of `==`/`bytes.Equal` to
  avoid timing attacks on signature verification.
- Concept: context propagation as the mechanism for "middleware learned X, a handler
  three layers down needs X" — `context.WithValue` + an unexported key type to avoid
  cross-package key collisions.

================================================================
3. WIDGET REGISTRY & INTERFACE
================================================================
- Design decision (deliberated, not assumed): kept a narrow interface
  (`Validate`/`Hit`/`State` returning a fixed `WidgetState{count, capped, remaining}`)
  scoped to counter-style widgets, rather than generalizing for hypothetical future
  widget types (poll, reactions) with no concrete second widget in hand yet. Reasoning:
  Go interfaces are cheap to widen later once a real second case exists to design
  against; guessing now would be pure speculation.
- `WidgetsRegistry` built as its own type (`Register`/`Get` methods) instead of a bare
  map field on `Application` — encapsulation boundary, even with only one widget type
  registered.
- Bugs/gaps found & fixed:
  - Registry had `Register` but no `Get` — callers reached directly into the internal
    map field, bypassing the abstraction that was supposedly the point of wrapping it.
  - `LikeWidget` originally embedded the *entire* `Application` (redis client, secret,
    even the registry itself) by value, when it only ever used `.redis`. Narrowed to
    just `redis *redis.Client` — smaller dependency surface, easier to test in
    isolation, correct dependency direction (widget depends on Redis, not on the app
    that depends on the widget).

================================================================
4. LIKE-BUTTON HIT LOGIC — accumulation, capping, validation
================================================================
- Core bug (most severe of the whole project so far): `Hit` correctly created state on
  the *first* hit but on every hit after that, just read back and returned the old
  state unchanged — the accumulate/clamp logic was never written for the "existing
  key" branch. The entire point of the widget (accumulate up to a cap) silently did
  nothing past hit #1.
- `redis.Nil` (key missing) vs a real connection error were being treated identically
  — a transient Redis error would be misread as "no prior data," at one point risking
  actual state resets on a lucky-timed failure.
- `WidgetState.Capped` was typed `int` and set to the cap *value* (20) instead of
  `bool` — broke the API contract silently (every response looked "capped" to a naive
  reader, or rather, always reported the wrong kind of value).
- Ignored errors from `redis.Set` and from the widget's own `Hit` call at the HTTP
  handler layer — failures were swallowed and reported as success.
- Critical security/business-logic bug: negative `count` values were accepted and fed
  into `min(count, remaining)` unmodified. Since `min` picks whichever number is
  smaller regardless of sign, a capped visitor could send `{"count": -20}` and
  literally subtract from their own stored count, resetting their cap and hitting
  again indefinitely — a complete bypass of the one hard requirement the whole
  project started with. Fixed with an explicit `count < 1` rejection using a sentinel
  error (`ErrInvalidCount`) checked via `errors.Is` in the handler, mapped to `400`.
- Concept: sentinel errors + `errors.Is` as the idiomatic way to let a lower layer
  (widget logic) communicate error *kind* up to a layer that decides HTTP status codes,
  without coupling the two together directly.

================================================================
5. GLOBAL COUNT + ATOMICITY (Lua scripting)
================================================================
- Realized `WidgetState.Count` was supposed to be the *global* public count per the
  written spec, but the implementation had been using the per-visitor tally for it
  the whole time — required splitting into two Redis keys: a permanent global count
  and a TTL'd per-visitor count, rather than one key serving both purposes.
- First attempt at "add Lua for atomicity" only wrapped the *write* in a script while
  still computing the clamped increment in Go from an earlier `MGet` snapshot — this
  reopened the exact race condition atomicity was meant to close, just moved it.
  Lesson: atomicity has to wrap read-compute-write as one unit; wrapping only the
  write buys nothing if the "how much to write" decision was already made outside it.
- Fixed by moving the read + clamp + write entirely inside one Lua script (`EVAL`),
  parameterized via `KEYS`/`ARGV`, so two concurrent hits from the same visitor can no
  longer both read stale state and each apply their own increment.
- Follow-on bugs from that change:
  - `MGET` does not return `redis.Nil` the way single-key `GET` does — missing keys
    just show up as `nil` entries in the result slice, no top-level error. A branch
    written assuming `errors.Is(err, redis.Nil)` after `MGet` was permanently dead
    code.
  - `State()` wasn't updated to match the new plain-integer storage format (it still
    tried `json.Unmarshal` on what was now a bare number) — silently returned zero
    state for every real visitor.
  - `State()` also had an *unchecked* type assertion (`countResults[0].(string)`)
    instead of the `v, ok := x.(string)` form — panicked on any visitor checking state
    before ever hitting (the normal first interaction with the widget).
  - The Lua script's `SET` on the session key had no expiry — the 30-day TTL
    (matching the anon-ID cookie's lifetime) silently disappeared, making the
    per-visitor key permanent again.
  - Passed `LIKE_TTL_PER_ANON_ID` (a `time.Duration`, i.e. nanoseconds) directly as a
    script argument used for `EXPIRE` — off by a factor of 1e9, would have set a TTL
    of tens of millions of years instead of 30 days. Fixed by converting to seconds
    explicitly before passing it in.
- Concept: know the difference between single-key and multi-key Redis command error
  semantics (`GET` vs `MGET`) — assuming they behave the same silently breaks error
  handling.

================================================================
6. CSRF PROTECTION — Origin-checking middleware
================================================================
- Concept, worth remembering: CORS and CSRF solve different problems. CORS only
  controls whether cross-origin JavaScript can *read* a response — it does not stop
  the browser from *sending* a request in the first place. A malicious page can still
  fire a cookie-carrying POST at an endpoint with no CORS headers at all; CORS being
  absent doesn't block the request, only reading its response. That's why Origin/
  Referer checking is a separate, necessary defense, independent of whatever CORS
  policy (or lack of one) exists.
- Design decisions made deliberately (not defaulted into):
  - Checked on every route except `/health` — a health check has no cookie-driven
    side effects, so there's nothing for a forged request to exploit there; blanket-
    applying the check would also break non-browser callers like uptime monitors
    that never send an `Origin` header, for zero security benefit.
  - Rejects on both a missing `Origin` header and a mismatched one (not just
    mismatched) — legitimate same-origin/cross-origin browser requests reliably send
    `Origin` on state-changing requests, so "missing" is itself a signal something's
    off (non-browser client, or an attack vector that doesn't set it).
  - `403 Forbidden`, not `400` — went back and checked the original spec, which had
    already specified this. `400` means "malformed request"; `403` means "understood
    fine, just not authorized" — the correct semantics for a trust decision.
  - Middleware ordered *before* `anonIDMiddleware` in the chain — reject cheaply
    before spending any work (cookie issuance, future Redis calls) on a request
    that's going to be thrown out anyway.
  - Origin value loaded from env (`ALLOWED_ORIGIN`), validated non-empty at startup —
    same pattern as `SECRET_KEY`. This also closes a subtle would-be bug: without
    that validation, an unset config value defaulting to `""` could accidentally
    match a request with no `Origin` header (`"" == ""`), silently disabling the
    entire check.
- Built a small `Chain(handler, middlewares...)` helper (unprompted, own initiative)
  to compose multiple middlewares in explicit, readable order instead of nesting
  function calls by hand (`a(b(c(handler)))`). Middlewares listed in the order they
  should execute, first-to-run first — cleaner than manual nesting once more than
  one middleware is involved.
- Bug found & fixed: the rejection response's `Content-Type` header was set *after*
  `WriteHeader(403)` was called — too late. In Go's `net/http`, headers must be set
  before `WriteHeader`, since that call locks in and flushes the header set. Same
  "order matters" lesson as the cookie-attribute work earlier in the project.
- Testing implication (same category as the `Secure`-cookie/Postman issue earlier):
  curl and Postman don't send an `Origin` header by default — every widget-route test
  now needs `-H "Origin: <ALLOWED_ORIGIN value>"` or it gets correctly `403`'d.

================================================================
7. RATE-LIMITING — two-tier (per-visitor + per-IP), Lua-backed counter
================================================================
- Design: kept the compound `(anonID, IP)` limit from the spec (1/sec, tuned to 2/sec
  in practice), but added a second, coarser IP-only limit (100/60s) as a backstop.
  Reasoning: the compound key relies on `anonID`, which `anonIDMiddleware` freely
  reissues to anyone who doesn't send a cookie back — so a hostile client that never
  persists its cookie gets a fresh identity (and a fresh rate-limit budget) on every
  single request, bypassing the per-visitor limit entirely. An IP-only limit doesn't
  depend on the client's cooperation, closing that specific bypass. A real browser
  cooperates automatically (persists cookies), so this only bites deliberately evasive
  clients.
- Chose a plain incrementing counter (Redis `INCR`-style via Lua) over a "true" token
  bucket — the actual requirement (flat N-per-window cap) doesn't need bucket/refill
  semantics, and the simpler mechanism generalizes cleanly: the strict "1/sec" compound
  limit turned out to just be this same counter mechanism with `cap = 1`, so one shared
  `checkRateLimit` helper serves both middlewares, parameterized by key/window/cap.
- Bugs found & fixed while writing the first version of the Lua script (three
  independent bugs, each alone enough to make *every* request fail closed):
  - `currentRateLimit += 1` — Lua has no compound assignment operators at all; this is
    a parse error, not a runtime one. Redis would refuse to load the script.
  - `if currentRateLimit >= max then` with `max` still a raw `ARGV` string, never
    passed through `tonumber`. Lua's arithmetic operators auto-coerce numeric strings,
    but its *relational* operators (`<`, `>=`, etc.) do not — comparing a number to a
    string with a relational operator raises a runtime error.
  - Returned Lua `true`/`false` from the script and tried to type-assert the Go result
    as `bool`. Redis's Lua-to-RESP conversion doesn't have a boolean type: Lua `false`
    becomes a RESP Nil reply, Lua `true` becomes the RESP integer `1` — so the reply
    is *never* actually a Go `bool`, and the assertion always failed. Fixed by having
    the script return plain integers (`0`/`1`) instead, sidestepping the conversion
    table entirely — general lesson: avoid returning Lua booleans from Redis scripts.
  - Follow-on: even after switching to integers, `res.(int)` was still wrong for the
    same reason `.(int64)` was required in the widget's own script earlier — Redis
    integer replies come back through go-redis as `int64`, never plain `int`. Used the
    `.Int()` convenience method instead of a manual type assertion, which is also
    simpler for a single scalar reply than what the widget script needed (that one
    returns a table, so it still needs manual `int64` assertions per element).
  - Copy-paste bug: the per-visitor middleware initially called `checkRateLimit` with
    the *IP-only* constants instead of its own — both limits were silently identical
    until caught.
- Verified deliberately, not assumed: whether this script's own `return` paths could
  ever yield `redis.Nil` (they can't — both branches always return a literal integer,
  unlike the internal `GET` inside the script, whose own possible Nil is already
  absorbed by `tonumber(... or 0)` before it affects the script's output).
- Concept: a non-nil `error` from a Redis call is not synonymous with "connection
  problem" — script compile errors and script runtime errors (exactly the three bugs
  above) surface through the same `error` value as network failures. Labeling error
  messages/logs as "connection error" when the real cause could just as easily be a
  bug in the script itself sends future debugging in the wrong direction.
- Open design question raised, not yet resolved: should a Redis failure make the
  rate-limiter fail *closed* (current behavior — Redis down means all traffic
  blocked) or fail *open* (Redis down means rate-limiting is skipped, prioritizing
  availability)? Left as a conscious open call rather than a default.
- Middleware ordering bug found & fixed: the IP-only limit was wired to run *before*
  CSRF in the chain, meaning a request CSRF would reject for free instead burned a
  Redis round-trip *and* consumed a slot from that IP's shared rate-limit budget
  first — letting an attacker exhaust a target IP's legitimate budget purely by
  flooding it with requests that were always going to be CSRF-rejected anyway.
  Reordered so the free check (CSRF) runs before the Redis-backed one.

================================================================
8. CORS — preflight handling + response headers
================================================================
- Discovered by testing first: `OPTIONS /widgets/like-widget/foo` returned `405
  Method Not Allowed`, not a 404 or a handler response. Root cause: Go 1.22+
  `ServeMux` dispatches by exact method+pattern — no `OPTIONS` route registered
  means the request never reaches any handler or middleware at all, including
  whatever CORS logic might already live inside a route's `Chain`. This ruled out
  "just add CORS headers inside the existing middleware chain" as a way to handle
  preflight — a *separate* route/handler was required.
- Design choice (deliberated): a single global `OPTIONS /` handler (Go's mux
  treats a bare `/` pattern as a catch-all subtree match) instead of registering
  an explicit `OPTIONS` route per path. Reasoning: one origin policy for the whole
  app, small route set, and a preflight approving a path that turns out not to
  exist is harmless — the real request still gets mux's normal 404 behavior
  afterward. Trade-off accepted: less precise per-path `Allow-Methods` metadata,
  but browsers only use preflight to decide *whether to send* the real request;
  actual enforcement still happens at real-request time regardless.
- Preflight response needs `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` in addition to
  `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`, because the real requests carry a credentialed
  cookie — first version omitted this, would have made the browser refuse the
  real request even after a "successful" preflight.
- Bugs found & fixed in the preflight handler itself:
  - No `Origin` validation at all initially — every preflight got a 200 approval
    regardless of requester, contradicting the CSRF-equivalent 403 policy already
    agreed on for mismatched origins.
  - `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` was a hardcoded string literal instead of
    `app.allowedOrigin` — config-driven design broken (changing the env var
    wouldn't change this response), and a second, independent source of truth for
    the same policy `csrfMiddleware` already enforces.
  - Fixed: read `r.Header.Get("Origin")`, compare against `app.allowedOrigin`,
    `403` on mismatch (mirrors `csrfMiddleware`'s check), only set the CORS
    headers + `200` on match.
- More significant bug: `corsMiddleware` (the one setting headers on *actual*,
  non-OPTIONS responses) was originally placed *last* in each route's `Chain`.
  Since `Chain`'s execution order matches the middleware list's order (first
  listed = outermost = runs first, per the existing `Chain` helper), any earlier
  middleware that short-circuited — `csrfMiddleware`'s 403, either rate-limit
  middleware's 429 — returned before ever reaching `corsMiddleware`. Result: every
  *rejected* request shipped back with no CORS headers at all, which browsers
  don't just ignore — a missing/mismatched `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` makes the
  browser refuse to let JS read the response body, turning a clean, intentional
  403/429 into an opaque, undebuggable "network error" client-side. Fixed by
  moving `corsMiddleware` to the front of every `Chain` list, so the header is set
  unconditionally before any other middleware gets a chance to exit early.
- Concept: CORS enforcement is about header presence/correctness, not status code
  — a 403 with the right `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` is still readable by
  browser JS; a 200 without it is not. Middleware that sets CORS headers has to
  run *before* anything that might return early, not after.

================================================================
9. SLUG VALIDATION — format + length, as middleware
================================================================
- Design: validate slug *format* before validating widget-type *existence* —
  reject cheaply (no Redis) before any lookup work. Implemented as
  `slugValidationMiddleware`, following the same `Chain` pattern as the other
  middlewares, wired only into the two routes that actually have a `{slug}`
  path segment (not `/health`).
- Regex: `^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$` — lowercase alphanumeric segments joined by
  single hyphens. Ended up stricter than the spec's original `[a-z0-9-]{1,100}`
  pattern: also rejects leading/trailing hyphens and double hyphens, which the
  simpler character-class version would have silently allowed.
- Confirmed `regexp.MustCompile` at package scope (compiled once at program
  start) is the right call over compiling per-request — `*regexp.Regexp` is safe
  for concurrent use across goroutines, and a panic on a malformed pattern at
  startup (fail-fast) is preferable to discovering it mid-request.
- The length-cap requirement (max 100 chars) went through three wrong attempts
  before landing correctly — all three were the same underlying misunderstanding
  (a quantifier on a *group* bounds how many times that group repeats, not how
  many characters get consumed):
  1. First version had no length bound at all — any shape-valid string of any
     length passed, a direct DoS surface (unbounded string ends up inside a
     Redis key and a Lua script argument).
  2. Changed the inner hyphen-group's quantifier from `*` to `{1,100}` — this
     bounds the *count of hyphen-separated segments* to 1–100, not total string
     length (a single 100-segment slug could still be enormous). It also
     introduced a regression: `{1,100}` requires *at least one* repetition, so a
     plain single-word slug with zero hyphens (e.g. `"cats"`) stopped matching
     entirely — a previously-valid case broken by a fix aimed at something else.
  3. Wrapped the *whole* original pattern in `{1,100}` instead — still didn't
     bound length. Regex engines are greedy: the first repetition of a
     variable-length group already consumes the entire input in one match (there's
     no delimiter forcing an early stop), so the outer repeat count never gets
     exercised in practice — functionally identical to no bound.
  - Fixed by reverting the regex to the original `*` form and adding a separate,
    explicit `len(slug) > MAX_SLUG_LENGTH` check in Go, independent of the regex
    match. Two simple, readable conditions instead of one regex trying to encode
    both a shape rule and a length rule.
- Missing-`return` bug, again: the first version of the length-check branch wrote
  the 400 response but had no `return` — fell through into the regex check and
  then into `next.ServeHTTP` regardless of outcome. An oversized-but-otherwise-
  valid slug got a 400 body written *and* still got passed to the real handler
  afterward (double response write, invalid request still served) — the exact
  same bug class as the very first fix of the whole project (`/health`, section 1),
  resurfacing verbatim in a brand-new file. Fixed by adding the `return`
  immediately after the length-check's error response.

================================================================
10. WEBSOCKET BROADCAST — live count updates, in-memory connection registry
================================================================
- Resolved (deliberate decision, recorded not just defaulted): rate-limiting on
  Redis failure stays fail-*closed* — matches what `checkRateLimit` already did
  (`err != nil` -> treated as limited/blocked). Trade-off acknowledged: a Redis
  outage takes the whole API down rather than silently disabling rate-limiting;
  accepted as the right call for this project.
- Design decisions made deliberately:
  - `gorilla/websocket` for the protocol layer — Go's stdlib has no WebSocket
    support at all.
  - Per-slug scope, not one global broadcast — a blog page only cares about the
    one widget instance it renders, no reason to fan out every widget's updates
    to every connection.
  - In-memory registry (`map[string]map[*websocket.Conn]bool`), no Redis Pub/Sub
    — consistent with the earlier "single instance" decision from the original
    spec discussion. A single process already holds every open connection in its
    own memory; Pub/Sub only earns its keep once there's a second process to fan
    out *to*. Same YAGNI reasoning as the widget-interface decision back in
    section 3.
  - Broadcast triggered from `hitLikeHandler` (HTTP layer), not from
    `LikeWidget.Hit` itself — keeps the widget's dependency surface unchanged
    (still only `*redis.Client`), same boundary reasoning as narrowing
    `LikeWidget` away from embedding the whole `Application` back in section 3.
  - Registry keyed by `widgetType + ":" + slug` (flat composite string), not
    nested per-type maps — caught *before* writing any code that slug alone
    isn't a safe key: two different widget types could share the same slug on
    the same blog post, exactly the reason `GenerateGlobalKey`/
    `GenerateSessionKey` already namespace their Redis keys by widget name.
    Flat vs. nested-map memory difference judged negligible at this scale;
    chosen for simpler locking and lookup (one map, one mutex, one hop).
- Bugs found & fixed (roughly in order found):
  - **Inverted helper function** — `isAllowedOrigin(r)` was written as
    `return origin != app.allowedOrigin`, i.e. it actually returned `true` when
    the origin *did not* match, exactly backwards from its own name. Shared
    between two call sites with opposite needs: `csrfMiddleware` happened to
    still work by accident (`if allowed { reject }` — "allowed" secretly meant
    "not allowed"), but gorilla's `Upgrader.CheckOrigin` contract is `true` =
    accept, so wired directly it rejected every legitimate same-origin
    connection and would have accepted forged cross-origin ones — the exact
    opposite of the intended security effect. Fixed by making the helper's
    return value match its name (`origin == app.allowedOrigin`) and adjusting
    `csrfMiddleware`'s condition instead.
  - **Gorilla's default `CheckOrigin` pitfall** (learned before it could bite):
    if `CheckOrigin` is left unset, gorilla's default compares `Origin` against
    the request's own `Host` header — which can never equal a cross-origin
    blog frontend's origin, so *every* legitimate connection would fail closed,
    not just malicious ones. `CheckOrigin` isn't optional here; it has to be
    set explicitly.
  - **Nil map write panic** — `app.clients[key][conn] = true` where `app.clients`
    was initialized as an empty outer map. For any `key` seen for the first
    time, `app.clients[key]` yields the zero value of a map type, which is
    `nil` — writing to a nil map panics at runtime. Fixed with a lazy-init
    check (`if _, ok := app.clients[key]; !ok { app.clients[key] = make(...) }`)
    before every write.
  - **Connection closed immediately after registering** — an early version of
    the WS handler registered the connection in the registry, then returned,
    with nothing blocking. Since `Upgrade()` hijacks the raw connection from
    `net/http`, returning from the handler doesn't itself close the socket, but
    an even earlier version had `defer conn.Close()` right after `Upgrade`,
    which *did* tear the connection down the instant the handler returned —
    meaning every connection died before any broadcast could ever reach it.
    Root issue: nothing was keeping the handler's goroutine alive for the
    connection's actual lifetime.
  - **Missing read loop** — even after removing the premature close, nothing
    called `conn.ReadMessage()` at all. Consequence: client disconnects were
    never detected server-side (no way to know the socket died until a future
    broadcast's write happened to fail against it), and client-sent WS
    ping/close control frames were never processed (gorilla only handles those
    from inside `ReadMessage`/`NextReader`). Fixed by adding a loop —
    `for { _, _, err := conn.ReadMessage(); if err != nil { ...cleanup...; break } }`
    — at the end of the handler; the handler's own goroutine (already provided
    for free by `net/http`, one per request) simply blocks there for the
    connection's whole lifetime instead of returning immediately. No extra
    goroutine, no channel needed for this — the request goroutine *is* the
    connection's goroutine once it's hijacked.
  - **No cleanup on disconnect** — the read loop, once added, broke out on
    error but didn't remove the dead connection from the registry or close it.
    Fixed by deleting the entry (under the same mutex used everywhere else) at
    the exact point the read error is detected, plus a `defer conn.Close()`
    right after a successful `Upgrade()` to guarantee the socket closes on
    every exit path.
  - **Unsynchronized map read racing a synchronized write** — in
    `hitLikeHandler`'s broadcast code, `clients := app.clients[key]` was read
    *before* `mutex.Lock()`, while `widgetWsHandler` only ever wrote to
    `app.clients` *inside* the lock. Two goroutines touching the same Go map
    without synchronization is undefined behavior — and unlike an ordinary
    panic (which `net/http` recovers from per-request), a detected concurrent
    map read/write is a *fatal* runtime error that takes the entire process
    down, not just one request. Fixed by moving the lookup inside the locked
    section, matching where the write already happened.
- Verified, not assumed: deleting from a Go map while ranging over it (done in
  the broadcast loop, `delete(clients, client)` on a failed write) is explicitly
  defined-safe by the language spec — no need to collect keys first and delete
  after the loop.
- Noted but deliberately deferred (YAGNI, revisit only if real contention shows
  up): a single global `mutex` guards the entire connection registry, and
  `WriteJSON` calls happen while holding it — a slow/stuck client's blocking
  write can momentarily stall broadcasts and connects for *every* widget, not
  just its own slug. Two known fixes discussed and shelved: a per-write
  `SetWriteDeadline` (bounds the stall, minimal change) or a per-connection
  outbound channel with its own writer goroutine (fully decouples broadcast
  from network speed, more moving parts — the standard production pattern for
  this). Skipped for now given this project's expected connection count.
- Concept: a WebSocket handshake is a plain HTTP request and is *not* subject to
  the browser's Same-Origin Policy the way `fetch`/XHR are — a page from any
  origin can attempt to open a WS connection to any server by default. CORS
  headers do nothing to stop this. Origin validation for WS has to be enforced
  explicitly server-side (`Upgrader.CheckOrigin`), independent of whatever CORS
  policy exists for regular HTTP responses.

================================================================
STILL OPEN / NOT YET BUILT
================================================================
- `LikeWidget.Validate` — still a stub (`return nil` always), never actually
  called from the HTTP handlers. (Slug format/length is now enforced, but via a
  separate middleware, not through this interface method — worth revisiting
  whether `Validate` should be removed or actually wired up.)
- Slow-WS-client write-blocking mitigation (write deadline or per-connection
  writer goroutine) — deliberately deferred, see section 10.

================================================================
RECURRING LESSONS (cross-cutting, worth remembering)
================================================================
- Trace concurrency scenarios by hand before trusting "it looks atomic" — atomicity
  is about *where* the read-compute-write boundary is drawn, not whether Lua/locks
  are present somewhere nearby.
- A function signature returning a plain `error` carries no information about *kind*
  of failure — sentinel errors + `errors.Is` is the idiomatic fix when a caller needs
  to react differently to different failure types.
- Default Go behaviors are usually "do nothing extra": no timeout, no cancellation,
  no status code override, no early return — all have to be added explicitly, and
  forgetting one is an easy, repeatable class of bug.
- When changing a data format (JSON blob -> plain int in Redis), audit *every* reader
  of that data, not just the writer you're actively editing.
- Type assertions: always use the `v, ok := x.(T)` form for anything derived from
  external/optional input (Redis replies, cookies, HTTP bodies) — the single-value
  form panics on mismatch.
- "Missing `return` after writing an error response" is not a one-off bug fixed
  once in `/health` — it resurfaced verbatim in slug validation, a completely
  different file, much later. Treat it as a reflex to check on every early-exit
  branch, not a solved problem tied to one piece of code.
- A quantifier applied to a variable-length group (`{1,100}`, `*`, etc.) bounds
  how many times that group repeats — it does not bound how many characters get
  consumed. When the actual goal is "string no longer than N chars," reach for
  `len()`, not a repetition count on part of the regex.
- Middleware that must run unconditionally (setting a header every response
  needs, e.g. CORS) has to sit *first* in the chain — anything placed after a
  middleware that can return early will simply never execute on that request.
- A function's name is a promise about what its return value means — a helper
  called `isAllowedOrigin` that actually returns "is *not* allowed" will keep
  working by accident at one call site and invert the security decision at the
  next. When reusing a boolean helper across call sites with different
  expectations, re-verify what `true`/`false` mean at *each* site, don't trust
  the name.
- A Go map's zero value is `nil`, and a nil map is read-safe but write-unsafe —
  `m[k]` on a missing key in a map-of-maps returns a nil inner map silently
  (no panic), but writing into that nil map panics immediately. Any "map of
  maps" needs an explicit exists-check-then-create before every write into the
  inner map.
- Concurrent, unsynchronized access to a plain Go map is worse than a normal
  bug: the runtime can detect it and raise a *fatal* error that kills the whole
  process, bypassing the usual per-request panic recovery `net/http` provides.
  Every read of shared mutable state needs the same lock discipline as every
  write to it — a lock around only the write half is not enough.
